  ✓ CONFIRMED: Uniswap buy path accepts zero minimum output
    Justification: Upon reviewing UniswapHandler.buyMalt in 
src/contracts/DexHandlers/UniswapHandler.sol, I confirmed that the alleged 
slippage protection vulnerability exists. The function obtains the handler's 
entire rewardToken balance, approves the router, and calls 
swapExactTokensForTokens with amountOutMin set explicitly to zero. Consequently,
any amount of Malt returned by the pool is accepted, subject only to the 
router's basic execution requirements. The deadline is now, but this does not 
constrain the execution price or prevent same-block transaction ordering and 
sandwich manipulation. StabilizerNode._startAuction invokes swingTrader.buyMalt 
before triggering an auction, while the trade size is calculated from pool 
reserves and a target price; neither that calculation nor 
StabilizerNode.stabilize supplies an execution-time minimum output to the 
handler. A searcher can therefore observe a pending authorized buy, trade 
against the Malt/reward pool to worsen the effective rate, allow the buy to 
execute, and potentially unwind the position afterward. The sellMalt function 
also uses amountOutMin zero, indicating the same missing control on the reverse 
path.
  ✗ REJECTED: RewardThrottle double-counts its entire token balance on rep
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged accounting error in 
RewardThrottle.handleReward, I determined this is a false positive under the 
normal ERC20 execution flow. Although the same-epoch branch adds the current 
balance to _state[_activeEpoch].profit, handleReward subsequently distributes 
the entire balance: either _sendToDistributor(balance, _activeEpoch) executes, 
or the overflow branch transfers the remainder to overflowPool and any remaining
amount to the distributor. Consequently, after a successful call, the contract 
should retain no previously accounted reward tokens, so a later call with no new
funding observes balance == 0 and does not increase profit. A later call with 
newly transferred tokens accounts for that new balance rather than 
double-counting the prior one. checkRewardUnderflow may itself request and 
distribute capital before this logic, but it likewise does not leave the prior 
balance retained. Exploitation would require a nonstandard or malicious token 
whose transfer does not reduce the contract balance, or reentrant behavior 
involving trusted external components, neither of which is established by the 
shown code. Therefore, the proposed repeated-call attack is not clearly 
exploitable.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Permissionless reserve-ratio sampling enables flash manipula
    Justification: Upon reviewing MaltDataLab.trackReserveRatio and 
LiquidityExtension.reserveRatio, I confirmed that the reported 
oracle-manipulation condition exists. trackReserveRatio is public and has no 
caller restriction, timing requirement, deviation bound, or trusted-source 
comparison. It obtains the ratio directly from 
liquidityExtension.reserveRatio(), which computes the value from the current 
Uniswap pair reserves returned by UniswapV2Library.getReserves. Those reserves 
can be temporarily distorted through a large swap or flash-swap transaction, 
after which the attacker can invoke trackReserveRatio while the manipulated 
state is observable, then unwind the trade in the same transaction. The sampled 
value is passed directly to reserveRatioMA.update without validation. 
StabilizerNode also invokes trackReserveRatio during stabilization paths, 
confirming that this data is part of protocol operations, although the supplied 
code does not include the moving-average implementation or every downstream 
consumer, so the ultimate economic impact and persistence constraints cannot be 
fully quantified. Existing role checks protect other sampling functions but do 
not protect this function, and no manipulation-resistant oracle mechanism is 
visible in the reviewed code.
  ✗ REJECTED: Observation History Can Be Distorted by Permissionless Stabi
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged observation-history distortion in 
AuctionBurnReserveSkew.addAbovePegObservation(), addBelowPegObservation(), and 
StabilizerNode.stabilize(), I determined that the hypothesis is not established 
as an exploitable vulnerability. The observation functions do ignore their 
amount arguments and increment the ring-buffer count for every successful 
observation, but StabilizerNode.stabilize() has no caller-supplied purchase 
amount. Instead, _distributeSupply() and _startAuction() derive trade sizes 
internally through dexHandler, and below-peg observations require purchaseAmount
to be at least one reward-token unit before an auction is triggered. 
Stabilization is permissionless, but it is protected by the stabilizeWindowEnd 
backoff and the _stabilityWindowOverride() timing and price-deviation checks; 
calls that do not meet _shouldAdjustSupply() merely track reserves and add no 
observation. Consequently, an attacker cannot simply submit repeated 
minimum-value observations or select arbitrary timing and direction through this
interface. The amount-insensitive accounting may be a design limitation, but the
claimed permissionless low-value attack path is not demonstrated by the provided
implementation.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Hardcoded Pair Init Code Hash Can Route Reserve Reads to a N
    Justification: Upon reviewing UniswapV2Library.pairFor and getReserves, I 
confirmed the integration error exists. pairFor deterministically computes the 
pair address using a fixed init-code hash (the hex value embedded in 
UniswapV2Library.sol) and performs no call to the configured factory's getPair 
function. This derived address is then used by getReserves, which directly 
invokes IUniswapV2Pair(pair).getReserves(). UniswapHandler.initialize and 
LiquidityExtension.initialize accept arbitrary factory addresses but do not 
validate the factory, its pair-code hash, or the existence and token composition
of the derived pair. Consequently, deploying either contract with a 
Uniswap-compatible fork or factory using different pair bytecode causes reserve 
reads to target an unrelated address or a nonexistent contract, potentially 
reverting all dependent pricing, reserve-ratio, and collateral-deficit 
operations. If an address containing compatible pair-like code happens to exist,
incorrect reserves could instead be consumed. The issue is conditional on 
privileged deployment misconfiguration rather than an unprivileged attacker, but
the source clearly lacks the required invariant validation.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: External Token Decimal Metadata Can Cause Arithmetic Reverts
    Justification: Upon reviewing the decimal-scaling logic in 
UniswapHandler.maltMarketPrice, MaltDataLab._normalizedPrice, and 
LiquidityExtension.collateralDeficit and reserveRatio, I confirmed that 
excessive token metadata can cause reserve-dependent calls to revert. These 
functions obtain decimals() from externally configured ERC20 contracts and use 
the result directly in 10**decimals and SafeMath multiplications, without 
validating a supported range. Although the OpenZeppelin ERC20 interface returns 
uint8, a token can still report a value such as 255, and the resulting 
exponentiation or subsequent multiplication by reserves will generally overflow 
or produce invalid scaling; malformed return data can also make the decimals() 
call revert. The same issue affects hasMinimumReserves through reserveRatio. 
Token addresses are supplied during privileged initialization, so this is not an
arbitrary permissionless attack against an already correctly configured 
deployment, but governance or deployment of a malicious/nonstandard token can 
permanently make public views and updater workflows unusable. No initialization 
or configuration guard rejects unsafe decimals values, and SafeMath only 
converts the overflow into a revert rather than preventing the denial of 
service.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: buyMalt Sweeps Pre-existing Malt Balance to the Buyer
    Justification: Upon reviewing UniswapHandler.buyMalt in 
src/contracts/DexHandlers/UniswapHandler.sol, I confirmed the accounting error 
exists. The function records the handler's reward-token balance, swaps that 
balance through the router with the Malt output sent to address(this), and then 
sets purchased = malt.balanceOf(address(this)) before transferring the entire 
value to msg.sender. It never records the Malt balance before the swap or 
calculates the post-swap increase. Consequently, any Malt already held by the 
handler—whether accidentally transferred, retained from another operation, or 
introduced through an integration flow—is swept to the authorized caller along 
with the current trade output. onlyRole(BUYER_ROLE) is a meaningful access 
control and prevents arbitrary public callers, but it does not distinguish 
legitimate purchased output from pre-existing inventory; therefore, a 
compromised, misconfigured, or otherwise authorized buyer can extract that 
inventory by calling buyMalt. The issue is not demonstrated in StabilizerNode 
itself, but the handler implementation clearly contains the vulnerable balance 
accounting and should use a before/after delta or reject nonzero pre-swap Malt 
balances.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Residual RewardToken Allowance Remains Authorized to Auction
    Justification: Upon reviewing StabilizerNode._distributeRewards and 
Auction.allocateArbRewards, I confirmed the residual-allowance vulnerability 
exists. StabilizerNode calls rewardToken.approve(address(auction), rewarded) 
before invoking auction.allocateArbRewards(rewarded), but never resets the 
allowance afterward or verifies the amount actually consumed. 
Auction.allocateArbRewards may return without transferring any tokens when the 
replenishing auction is invalid, has no commitments, or already has sufficient 
claimable tokens, leaving the full approval outstanding. Even when it transfers 
only a fraction through collateralToken.safeTransferFrom(stabilizerNode, 
address(this), maxArbAllocation), the unused allowance remains authorized. The 
allowance is not limited to the current transaction and can apply to reward 
tokens subsequently held by StabilizerNode. Auction's STABILIZER_NODE_ROLE check
restricts the intended implementation, but it does not protect against a 
compromised, replaceable, or otherwise malicious auction implementation using 
transferFrom. The existing code therefore creates a persistent authorization 
from StabilizerNode to the auction and should clear or tightly reconcile the 
allowance after allocation.
  ✗ REJECTED: Independent Reward-Cut Configuration Can Make Stabilization 
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged denial-of-service vulnerability in 
StabilizerNode._distributeRewards and setRewardCut, I determined this is a false
positive. _distributeRewards calculates the caller, LP, DAO, auction-pool, and 
swing-trader cuts and derives treasuryCut using SafeMath subtraction. However, 
setRewardCut explicitly computes the sum of all five configurable cuts with 
SafeMath and requires sum <= 1000 before updating any state. There are no 
separate setters that could bypass this aggregate validation, and the function 
also sets treasuryRewardCut to 1000 - sum. Consequently, each individual cut is 
bounded collectively to at most 100% of rewarded, while integer division can 
only reduce the aggregate calculated cuts through rounding; it cannot make them 
exceed rewarded. The subsequent subtraction therefore cannot revert because of 
an over-allocation caused by reward-cut configuration. Although an administrator
can intentionally configure values that cause other operational behavior, the 
specific hypothesized aggregate-subtraction denial of service is prevented by 
the setter-level invariant.
  ✗ REJECTED: Bonding State Is Updated After External Mining Call, Enablin
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged reentrancy in Bonding.bondToAccount, 
Bonding.unbond, Bonding.unbondAndBreak, and MiningService.onBond/onUnbond, I 
determined this is not a clearly exploitable vulnerability from the supplied 
code. MiningService does make external calls to configured reward mines before 
Bonding updates its accounting, and no explicit reentrancy guard exists. 
However, callbacks execute with the reward mine as msg.sender, not the original 
bonding user, so a callback cannot directly reenter unbond for that user. A 
malicious mine could only operate on its own bonded balance or invoke 
bondToAccount subject to its own token allowance and the notSameBlock checks. 
More importantly, _removeFromBonded uses SafeMath and updates the bonded balance
before transferring tokens; nested unbond operations that collectively exceed 
the recorded balance revert rather than over-withdraw. The unbondAndBreak path 
likewise updates accounting before its subsequent external calls. Therefore, 
although the interaction ordering is defensively undesirable and trusted reward 
mines should not call back into Bonding, the code does not demonstrate a 
practical balance-draining reentrancy exploit.
  ✗ REJECTED: Unbounded Outstanding-Auction Iteration Enables Claim/Replen
    Reason: Upon reviewing AuctionParticipant.claim and outstandingArbTokens, I 
determined that the alleged claim-path denial of service is a false positive. 
The claim function processes exactly one auction entry per transaction using 
auctionIds, and advances replenishingIndex only after that entry is fully 
claimable, so claim execution is bounded and resumable rather than scanning the 
entire array. outstandingArbTokens does contain an unbounded loop over 
auctionIds, but it is a view function and is not invoked by claim or by the 
shown participant processing path; excessive length can make RPC estimation or 
on-chain view calls expensive, but it cannot directly make state-changing claims
fail. Auction.allocateArbRewards contains a separate while loop that skips 
finalized auctions with zero commitments, but it is controlled by the privileged
stabilizer node and is not the outstanding-auction iteration described in the 
hypothesis. Although array growth and other unbounded view functions merit 
optimization, the supplied code does not establish an attacker-controlled 
transaction denial of service preventing claims or replenishment.
  ✗ REJECTED: Auction Start Gate Dependency Can Halt Auction Lifecycle
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged denial-of-service condition in 
StabilizerNode._startAuction and Auction._triggerAuction, I determined this is 
not a clearly exploitable vulnerability. Both functions treat the start 
controller as optional: they skip the external call when auctionStartController 
is address(0), and a controller returning false merely causes the function to 
return without starting an auction. A reverting controller can make 
StabilizerNode.stabilize revert, but the dependency is 
administrator-configurable. StabilizerNode.setAuctionStartController and 
Auction.setAuctionStartController both permit an ADMIN_ROLE holder to replace or
clear the controller, explicitly documenting that address(0) is valid. 
Auction.triggerAuction is additionally restricted to STABILIZER_NODE_ROLE, 
preventing arbitrary users from forcing the dependency path. Thus, a 
controller-induced halt would require privileged configuration or failure of an 
administrator-controlled component and has an existing recovery mechanism: 
clearing or replacing the controller in both contracts. Although no timeout or 
automated fallback exists, that is an operational resilience consideration 
rather than a demonstrated unprivileged denial-of-service vulnerability.
  ✗ REJECTED: Per-Transfer Pool Outflow Threshold Can Be Bypassed by Split
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged per-transfer pool outflow threshold 
bypass in PoolTransferVerification.verifyTransfer, 
TransferService.verifyTransfer, and Malt._beforeTokenTransfer, I determined this
is a false positive. The thresholdBps value is not an amount limit: 
verifyTransfer uses it solely to calculate a price condition, requiring 
maltDataLab.maltPriceAverage(priceLookback) to exceed priceTarget multiplied by 
(10000 - thresholdBps) / 10000. The amount parameter is accepted and forwarded 
through TransferService, but is never read by PoolTransferVerification, so there
is no per-transfer amount threshold that can be bypassed by splitting transfers.
Transfers from the configured pool are checked on every token operation through 
Malt._beforeTokenTransfer, unless the recipient is whitelisted. Although the 
implementation has no cumulative outflow accounting, that omission does not 
establish the claimed vulnerability because the configured threshold governs 
price deviation rather than aggregate or individual transfer volume. A separate 
aggregate pool-drain concern would require evidence that thresholdBps was 
intended to limit transfer amounts, which is not supported by this source code.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Zero Sample Length Can Permanently Revert Moving-Average Upd
    Justification: Upon reviewing the MovingAverage initializer, configuration 
methods, and arithmetic paths, I confirmed that zero sample length can put the 
contract into a denial-of-service state when supplied during initialization. 
initialize() validates _sampleMemory but does not validate _sampleLength before 
storing it, whereas setSampleLength() later correctly requires _sampleLength > 
0. With sampleLength equal to zero, getValueWithLookback() immediately executes 
modulo sampleLength and reverts. Subsequent update() calls also encounter 
invalid zero-length arithmetic, including (sampleLength - 1) under SafeMath and 
divisions by sampleLength in _createNewSample(); updateCumulative() similarly 
reaches division by sampleLength or other zero-length logic. MaltDataLab 
forwards tracking operations directly to these update functions, so affected 
oracle updates can revert, and its smoothed-read methods can revert through 
getValueWithLookback(). The issue is limited to an administrator or deployment 
process supplying zero during initialize(), since the normal setSampleLength() 
setter rejects zero, and it is recoverable by setting a positive value. 
Nevertheless, the missing initialization guard provides a clear misconfiguration
or compromised-administrator DoS path.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Registered Reward Mine Revert Can Denial-of-Service Bonding 
    Justification: Upon reviewing MiningService.onBond and onUnbond and 
Bonding.bondToAccount, unbond, and unbondAndBreak, I confirmed the 
denial-of-service condition exists. MiningService iterates over every address in
mines and synchronously invokes IRewardMine(mine).onBond or onUnbond without 
checking mineActive, using try/catch, imposing gas bounds, or isolating 
failures. Any revert from one registered mine therefore reverts the entire 
MiningService call. Bonding invokes these callbacks before updating user 
balances or transferring tokens, so a reverting mine prevents all bonding and 
unbonding operations that depend on the affected MiningService. addRewardMine 
accepts an arbitrary address and performs no interface or behavioral validation,
making faulty or malicious implementations registrable by an administrator. 
Although removeRewardMine provides an administrative recovery mechanism, it does
not prevent the disruption and may be unavailable during an administrative 
compromise or operational failure. The hypothesis is therefore technically 
valid, with exploitability depending on a mine being registered and made to 
revert; this is not a permissionless attack against the current code unless 
administrative control or a faulty registered mine is involved.
  ✗ REJECTED: Unbounded SwingTrader Profit Cut Can Halt Profit Distributio
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged denial-of-service vulnerability in 
SwingTrader.setLpProfitCut and SwingTrader.sellMalt, I determined this is a 
false positive. The administrator-controlled lpProfitCut is explicitly 
constrained by require(_profitCut >= 0 && _profitCut <= 1000, "Must be between 0
and 100%"); at the setter, where 1000 is the denominator used by 
profit.mul(lpProfitCut).div(1000). Because uint256 values are nonnegative, the 
lower-bound check is redundant, but the upper-bound check prevents values above 
100%. In sellMalt, a 0% cut results in a zero transfer, while a 100% cut 
transfers the calculated profit to rewardThrottle; neither boundary inherently 
reverts. The distribution arithmetic therefore cannot be forced to underflow 
through an excessive configured profit cut. Although SafeMath multiplication 
could theoretically overflow for an extraordinarily large profit amount, that is
unrelated to an unbounded administrator parameter and is constrained by 
available token balances. No clear configuration-based path exists whereby 
setting lpProfitCut causes StabilizerNode stabilization or profit distribution 
to revert. The hypothesis is consequently not supported by the provided 
implementation.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: RewardReinvestor Can Attribute Pre-existing Reward Balance t
    Justification: Upon reviewing the RewardReinvestor functions 
provideReinvest, splitReinvest, _retrieveReward, and _bondAccount, I confirmed 
the accounting error is exploitable. Both public reinvestment paths withdraw 
rewards for msg.sender, but then calculate or transfer the contract’s entire 
rewardToken balance rather than the amount received during that call. In 
provideReinvest, rewardBalance is set directly to 
rewardToken.balanceOf(address(this)); in splitReinvest, half of the entire 
balance is sent to dexHandler and _bondAccount subsequently transfers the 
remaining entire balance. _bondAccount supplies all contract-held assets to 
dexHandler and bonds the resulting liquidity using 
bonding.bondToAccount(account), where account is msg.sender. Therefore, an 
attacker can ensure reward tokens are already held by RewardReinvestor, call 
either function with a positive rewardLiquidity corresponding to their own 
rewards, and receive credit for liquidity generated from both their withdrawal 
and the pre-existing balance. The only guard rejects zero withdrawals and does 
not isolate the withdrawal delta. Any residual assets are swept to treasury only
after they have been included in the attacker’s bond.


